The progression proves finite real vector identities and inequalities, rectangular map/transpose relations, reversible elimination and complete solution families, image/row/kernel bases and rank–nullity, unnormalized orthogonalization and its skip-and-continue dependent-column case, conditional full-column-rank QR, and least-squares necessity/sufficiency by a squared-norm expansion. A geometric construction proves projection existence and unique fitted output for any finite matrix, including rank zero. Coefficients are unique exactly for independent columns; otherwise every minimizer is a particular one plus the kernel. Coordinate changes, residual metrics, extra coefficient preferences, input sensitivity and floating stability remain distinct questions.

The original model has rows (1,0,1), (0,1,1), (1,1,2), (2,−1,1) and readings b=(2,1,4,2). Its rank is 2, its kernel is spanned by (−1,−1,1), and exact elimination exposes contradictory zero-coefficient rows. With u=x₁+x₃ and v=x₂+x₃, the unweighted fit has u=33/17 and v=28/17, fitted output (33,28,61,38)/17, residual (1,−11,7,−4)/17 and squared error 11/17. Every (33/17−t,28/17−t,t) gives that same fit. The consistent alternative (2,1,3,3) has exact solutions (2−t,1−t,t).

Changing the image basis to (c₁,c₁+c₂) changes coordinates to (5/17,28/17) while preserving the fit. A fifth row directly observing x₃=1/2 removes coefficient ambiguity: the fitted vector becomes (49/34,39/34,1/2), the original residual remains and its squared norm stays 11/17. Doubling the third row and its reading changes the residual metric; the new fit has u=51/25, v=91/50 and weighted squared error 41/50. An independent quadratic argument explains each change. The ε-matrix example proves absolute amplification 1/ε in a specified right-hand-side direction and maximum norms, with relative ratio (2+ε)/ε; this is not a worst-case condition-number or floating-stability result. The model is stipulated and dimensionless, not a measured instrument.

