The block develops quantified sequence convergence, real completeness as an explicit foundational premise, monotone and Cauchy convergence, partial sums, geometric and telescoping remainders, divergence and comparison bounds. Conditional order is demonstrated through finite harmonic identities, while absolute convergence supplies the permitted permutation argument. Uniform convergence is distinguished from pointwise convergence using continuity, spike and derivative counterexamples. The sufficient derivative-limit theorem is proved using the fundamental theorem of calculus. Existing finite Taylor remainder and exponential/logarithm definitions are reused to justify bounded-interval representations; a smooth nonanalytic function prevents conflating a convergent Taylor series with its originating function.

The original investigation groups ordered nonnegative pairs by n=i+j with weight r^n, explicitly restricted to 0≤r<1. Counts alone do not assign physical probabilities. It derives the normalizer 1/(1−r)², moments, exact cutoff tails, conditional renormalization, total variation and a first-moment error certificate. Omitted probability alone cannot bound an unbounded observable. At r=1/2 and cutoff 3, omitted mass is 3/16, omitted first moment is 1 and the conditional mean is 16/13 versus the full mean 2. The first cutoff satisfying the stated first-moment certificate at tolerance 1/100 is 13; this is a certificate criterion, not a universal minimum for actual error. The independent extension changes the state count to three coordinates and requires a fresh proof, implementation, criticism and revision. No physical ensemble, fitted distribution, learner study or experiment is claimed.

